WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.150
Before he ever picked up a lightsaber, Eman
Esfandi was firing away precision strikes

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:06.846
with a very different implement.

00:00:06.846 --> 00:00:11.849
From showing up in random commercials to throwing
down at slam poetry shows, here's what you

00:00:11.849 --> 00:00:15.019
didn't know about the "Ahsoka" star.

00:00:15.019 --> 00:00:19.289
Eman Esfandi grew up in the small Texas border
city of Laredo, which is known for having

00:00:19.289 --> 00:00:25.000
the highest proportion of Hispanic Americans
of any city in the United States outside of Puerto Rico.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.910
This lack of ethnic diversity proved challenging
for Esfandi, whose mother hails from Ecuador

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:31.695
and whose father comes from Iran.

00:00:31.695 --> 00:00:34.200
As he explained to The Daily Texan in 2022,

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:40.000
"I was around zero Persian people my whole
life, mostly around Mexican and Caucasian people.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.050
Texas Caucasian people can be really rough,
especially the years I was growing up.

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:48.680
Very country, homophobic, Islamophobic, [and]
brutal."

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.150
And in an interview with Flaunt, Esfandi recalled,

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:54.930
"[I] grew up with the repercussions of 9/11
all throughout my childhood.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:59.050
When I was younger it wasn't as noticeable,
but by the time I was in high school, I could

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:04.790
better see the microaggressions that my being
half-Persian, or my father being from Iran, would incite."

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:08.930
Esfandi would later utilize this racism to
help fuel his career.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:13.220
Esfandi's parents have been business owners
in Laredo for most of their son's life.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:17.530
He spent a considerable amount of his childhood
in the family shop and even hoped to one day

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:18.943
follow in their footsteps.

00:01:18.943 --> 00:01:23.400
So he got an associate's degree from his hometown
school of Laredo College, and then he later

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:28.400
graduated from the University of Texas with
a Bachelor's in Economics with the expectation

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.892
of running a business similar to his parents'
shop.

00:01:30.892 --> 00:01:34.532
But then he changed his focus when he discovered
a passion for film and art.

00:01:34.532 --> 00:01:37.170
As he recalled to Shoutout LA in 2021,

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:42.240
"I wasn't taught how to express myself, didn't
have any examples of how to become an artist.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.600
I'm grateful though, for it's the stark contrast
of my upbringing to my current lifestyle.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:50.869
It's humbling and empowering to remind myself
that I started from nothing and wasn't handed

00:01:50.869 --> 00:01:52.590
things on a silver platter."

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:56.909
There was yet another completely different
life trajectory that Esfandi almost ended

00:01:56.909 --> 00:02:00.320
up on, as he was once considered to be a bit
of a tennis prodigy.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:04.420
He took up the sport at the age of 13 and
spent his high school senior year practicing

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:07.973
at the John Newcombe Tennis Academy in New
Braunfels, Texas.

00:02:07.973 --> 00:02:11.720
He was even at one point ranked in the top
five in the country while playing collegiate

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:13.493
tennis at Laredo College.

00:02:13.493 --> 00:02:17.840
Esfandi kept playing for years, and he eventually
became a mentor and an instructor for tennis

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.210
prospects of all ages as recently as 2020.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:25.910
His athletic background came in handy in 2021
when he landed a small role in the movie King

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:31.209
Richard, which tells the story of Richard
Williams, father of the legendary Venus and Serena.

00:02:31.209 --> 00:02:32.923
"This guy, Richard Williams, keeps calling.

00:02:32.923 --> 00:02:34.882
It's like the tenth time in a row."

00:02:34.882 --> 00:02:39.110
Esfandi remains obsessed with the game, as
he regularly shares highlights of his practices

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:40.769
and matches on Instagram.

00:02:40.769 --> 00:02:46.050
With a sports background and a fine education,
it wasn't exactly the obvious choice for Eman

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:48.352
Esfandi to dive headfirst into acting.

00:02:48.352 --> 00:02:51.541
But he found his own direction in life by
taking a unique route.

00:02:51.541 --> 00:02:54.134
As he explained to Voyage LA in 2019,

00:02:54.134 --> 00:02:58.430
"I had been conducting impromptu 'interviews'
at coffee shops around Austin with anyone

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:02.330
who was interested in starting up a conversation
[...] It was my way to research different

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:05.360
life paths without having to experience them
myself."

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:08.610
Esfandi eventually took his project to a local
acting workshop.

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:09.610
As he recalled,

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:14.099
"I did, in fact, meet people who had drastically
changed their life paths, but another interesting

00:03:14.099 --> 00:03:15.099
thing also happened.

00:03:15.099 --> 00:03:18.209
I ended up volunteering to work through a
couple of scenes."

00:03:18.209 --> 00:03:22.180
Esfandi impressed the casting directors who
were operating the workshop and was encouraged

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:24.930
to find a manager and pursue an acting career.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:29.470
So he started attending workshops around Austin
and landed some substantial roles in student

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:35.640
projects and short films before fully committing
by making the move to Los Angeles in 2018.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:40.522
Like plenty of other actors, Eman Esfandi
got his start on screen with some TV commercial gigs.

00:03:40.522 --> 00:03:45.240
Impressively enough, he landed his first nationally
televised campaign shortly after attending

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:46.989
his first acting workshop.

00:03:46.989 --> 00:03:50.659
Since then, Esfandi has managed to book a
veritable slew of ads, for the likes of such

00:03:50.659 --> 00:03:54.130
companies as Amazon, Bose, and H&amp;R Block.

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:55.130
One spot for L.L.

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:59.069
Bean features him sleeping on top of a ladder
of hammocks as he wishes his camping mates

00:03:59.069 --> 00:04:00.069
a good morning.

00:04:00.069 --> 00:04:04.209
He also appeared in Coca-Cola's "Food Feud"
campaign, which features people making their

00:04:04.209 --> 00:04:08.459
cases for various meals as the optimal thing
to eat while watching football.

00:04:08.459 --> 00:04:11.980
Esfandi plays a guy who stumps for a certain
type of iconic sandwich.

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:13.370
"Dude, subs."

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:17.690
For many actors, it's a natural evolution
to one day sit in the director's chair and

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.180
run the show behind the camera.

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:23.350
Eman Esfandi has actually already made this
transition, as he's an award-winning director

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:24.460
of short films.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:29.509
His first and most notable stint as a director
came as a passion project alongside his childhood

00:04:29.509 --> 00:04:30.905
friend, Isaac Garza.

00:04:30.905 --> 00:04:36.150
Garza approached Esfandi about helping him
produce an absurdist comedy short titled Pepito.

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:39.900
With Garza writing and starring, he invited
Esfandi to be the director.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:44.350
So Esfandi taught himself how to direct by
devouring a crash course of YouTube videos,

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:46.880
and then he took the helm for the three-day
shoot.

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:51.820
To their surprise, Pepito went on to be selected
as a winner of the Latinx Short Film Festival

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:57.120
in 2019, and it also earned a nationwide distribution
deal on HBO platforms.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:01.790
Esfandi followed up that triumph by directing
another short film called 120, which premiered

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:03.428
at the Cannes Short Film Corner.

00:05:03.428 --> 00:05:07.410
Since then, though, he's pretty much abandoned
the director's chair, as he's chosen instead

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:11.090
to focus on his acting and other creative
pursuits.

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:15.840
The COVID-19 pandemic left a huge percentage
of the world's population to their own devices

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:17.363
while they were locked up in quarantine.

00:05:17.363 --> 00:05:22.180
Eman Esfandi decided to use this isolation
to his advantage by pursuing artistic outlets

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:23.514
beyond filmmaking.

00:05:23.514 --> 00:05:26.344
This included discovering a love for slam
poetry.

00:05:26.344 --> 00:05:30.280
Slam poetry involves performing an original
piece in front of an audience that's encouraged

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:33.333
to participate with finger snaps or words
of affirmation.

00:05:33.333 --> 00:05:37.720
This eccentric genre of live entertainment
is fitting for someone like Esfandi, as it's

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:42.419
often treated as a highly emotional monologue
that relies on the performer's individual style.

00:05:42.419 --> 00:05:47.070
He's continued to practice this unique art
form, including in March 2023 when he shared

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:51.884
an Instagram reel of himself performing a
rendition of his poem "I'm Tired.”

00:05:51.884 --> 00:05:54.115
"You good?

00:05:54.585 --> 00:05:57.073
Hey you good?"

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:02.060
It was a natural progression for Esfandi to
adapt his passions for poetry and performing

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:03.444
into a music career.

00:06:03.444 --> 00:06:07.900
Under the stage name of DragonWulf, he spent
much of his time in quarantine learning everything

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:10.000
he could about producing his own songs.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:13.309
As he explained to the Laredo Morning Times
in 2023,

00:06:13.309 --> 00:06:17.410
"I learned so much about music over the course
of a year [...] I realized I want to do this

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:19.020
all the way and forever."

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:24.340
Esmandi released his first DragonWulf EP in
September 2020, though he eventually decided

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:26.030
to make it no longer available.

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:27.030
As he explained,

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:30.600
"I took a more professional approach to learning
it all [...] while carving out my style and

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.055
figuring out what it is I wanted to do with
the music."

00:06:33.055 --> 00:06:37.460
Esfandi then released a new single, "You're
Perfect in Every Way," and he's since followed

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:43.060
that up with three albums, A DragonWulf Summer,
La Vie En Vert, and La Sagrada Familia.

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:45.922
Esfandi has looked back on his musical journey
by reflecting,

00:06:45.922 --> 00:06:49.360
"It was a lot of self and inner work and discovery
and expression.

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:55.428
It broke through a bunch of barriers of fear,
anxiety, and insecurity and eradicated all that."

00:06:55.428 --> 00:07:00.120
Working on something as big as Ahsoka is sure
to help Esfandi land himself some higher profile

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:02.084
projects in the near future.

00:07:02.084 --> 00:07:05.694
But he's actually already built himself a
pretty solid filmography.

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:10.750
His onscreen credits date back to 2017, when
he starred in an assortment of short films

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:13.639
with names like Death by Script and Mother
of the Golds.

00:07:13.639 --> 00:07:18.120
By 2018, Esfandi hooked up with fellow Star
Wars veteran Robert Rodriguez.

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:22.120
The actor starred in Rodriguez's low-budget
flick Red 11, which had its world premiere

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:25.419
at the 2019 SXSW Film Festival.

00:07:25.419 --> 00:07:30.050
Esfandi then made his silver screen debut
in 2021's King Richard before taking a more

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:33.270
substantial role in 2022's The Inspection.

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:37.470
It was a part that really hit close to home,
as he played a Muslim-American man recruited

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:41.158
into the Marines during the height of post-9/11
Islamophobia.

00:07:41.158 --> 00:07:43.080
"Have you ever been convicted of a felony?

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:44.435
Are you communist?"

00:07:44.435 --> 00:07:48.610
As impressive as Esfandi's rise through the
Hollywood ranks has been, he's still faced

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:53.520
some adversity, particularly when it comes
to the type of roles he gets offers for.

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:57.970
Since moving to Los Angeles in 2018, he's
gone out on plenty of auditions.

00:07:57.970 --> 00:08:02.199
Unfortunately, the parts that he generally
gets the most calls for are stereotypical,

00:08:02.199 --> 00:08:06.500
and frequently racist, characters that seem
to be stuck on his Iranian heritage.

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:09.133
As he noted to The Daily Texan in 2022,

00:08:09.309 --> 00:08:11.780
"I'm not going to audition for something that's
offensive.

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:14.069
I'm not perfect for [a role] because I'm brown.

00:08:14.069 --> 00:08:17.139
I'm not perfect for that because I can play
this side character.

00:08:17.139 --> 00:08:20.310
I'm perfect for real characters because I'm
a real person."

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:24.069
Esfandi also pointed out that his role in
The Inspection was a good example of the sort

00:08:24.069 --> 00:08:25.629
of character he prefers to play.

00:08:25.629 --> 00:08:31.039
In that film, he was able to represent both
his Persian background and his Ecuadorian heritage.

00:08:31.039 --> 00:08:34.990
Meanwhile, the part also allowed him to explore
and confront much of the racism that he's

00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:36.738
endured throughout his life.

00:08:36.738 --> 00:08:42.000
It's difficult for Esfandi to ignore how meteorically
his career has taken off by playing a fan-favorite

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:45.190
character in one of the world's biggest franchises.

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:48.010
But that doesn't mean that this development
is a total surprise to him.

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:51.970
He credits the power of manifestation as a
major reason for his success.

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:56.010
It's also a huge influence on his music, as
the power of belief has helped him turn his

00:08:56.010 --> 00:08:57.315
dreams into reality.

00:08:57.315 --> 00:08:59.717
As he explained to The Laredo Morning Times,

00:08:59.717 --> 00:09:03.269
"I'm rapping from a place in the future that
I'm adopting the feeling of and calling that

00:09:03.269 --> 00:09:08.090
into my life [...] I dropped A DragonWulf
Summer, and it's very flex rap and extravagant.

00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:09.090
I'm flexing.

00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:10.625
And that's when I booked Star Wars.

00:09:10.625 --> 00:09:13.234
It doesn't get more obvious that these concepts
work."

00:09:13.234 --> 00:09:14.519
Esfandi also noted,

00:09:14.519 --> 00:09:18.700
"I know how the universe works on a spiritual
level, and I know I can get a hold of it on

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:23.030
a physical, tangible level [...] If anything,
at the end of this whole thing, I'll be living

00:09:23.030 --> 00:09:26.407
proof and these songs will be my retrospect."

00:09:26.407 --> 00:09:30.870
It's no secret that the Star Wars fandom can
be quite finicky when new characters, or new

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:33.950
versions of established characters, enter
the franchise.

00:09:33.950 --> 00:09:37.530
All you have to do is go anywhere on the Internet
where Star Wars fans gather to understand

00:09:37.530 --> 00:09:39.425
how difficult they can be to please.

00:09:39.425 --> 00:09:43.779
But plenty of viewers seem to be embracing
Esfandi as the live-action choice for beloved

00:09:43.779 --> 00:09:45.779
Rebels hero Ezra Bridger.

00:09:45.779 --> 00:09:49.380
So it might be a little surprising to learn
that Esfandi had some serious competition

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:51.083
during the casting process.

00:09:51.083 --> 00:09:55.190
Many actors expressed interest in the role
for Ahsoka, and one of the leading names rumored

00:09:55.190 --> 00:09:58.900
for the part was the lead of the live-action
Aladdin, Mena Massoud.

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:03.940
Massoud even quoted Ezra on an Instagram post
back in 2021, which fueled speculation that

00:10:03.940 --> 00:10:05.640
he was close to nabbing the part.

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:09.390
Massoud inevitably had to address the casting
decision when it was announced that Esfandi

00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:10.399
won the part.

00:10:10.399 --> 00:10:12.260
In a since-deleted tweet, he wrote,

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:13.899
"Glad the rumors will stop now.

00:10:13.899 --> 00:10:17.970
Never really had a fair shot at it, unfortunately
[...] just wasn't meant for me, I guess.

00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:20.750
Can't deny the dude looks exactly like Ezra."

00:10:20.750 --> 00:10:24.783
It all seems to have worked out for the best,
as Esfandi is flourishing in the role, leading

00:10:24.783 --> 00:10:29.701
to rumors that Ezra Bridger may get his very
own Disney+ series in the near future.

